tion Profile","statement":"Placeholder profile for District of Columbia insurance restoration regulations, building codes, and carrier practices. Requires verification against official state sources.","interpretation":"Atlas jurisdiction data for District of Columbia (DC) is currently a placeholder. Actual regulations, building codes, licensing requirements, and carrier-specific practices for District of Columbia must be verified against official state sources before being used in operational decisions.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"atlas-curated","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States > District of Columbia","confidence":0.3,"isInference":true,"status":"active","tags":["jurisdiction","placeholder","dc","state"],"verificationStatus":"placeholder","corpusVersion":"1.0.0"},{"id":"std_iicrc_s500","knowledgeType":"standard","title":"IICRC S500 — Standard for Professional Water Damage Restoration","statement":"IICRC S500 establishes the standards and procedures for water damage restoration, including assessment, moisture detection, structural drying, and documentation requirements.","interpretation":"S500 is the primary standard for water mitigation claims. Compliance with S500 procedures strengthens the justification for mitigation charges and drying equipment days.","sourceClassification":"INDUSTRY_STANDARD","sourceId":"iicrc-s500","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.9,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["iicrc","s500","water","standard","mitigation"]},{"id":"std_iicrc_s520","knowledgeType":"standard","title":"IICRC S520 — Standard for Professional Mold Remediation","statement":"IICRC S520 establishes the standards and procedures for mold remediation, including assessment, containment, removal, and post-remediation verification.","interpretation":"S520 compliance is critical for mold claims. Deviation from S520 procedures can result in carrier disputes and potential liability.","sourceClassification":"INDUSTRY_STANDARD","sourceId":"iicrc-s520","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.9,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["iicrc","s520","mold","standard","remediation"]},{"id":"std_iicrc_s530","knowledgeType":"standard","title":"IICRC S530 — Standard for Professional Fire & Smoke Damage Restoration","statement":"IICRC S530 establishes standards for fire and smoke damage restoration, including assessment, cleaning, deodorization, and repair procedures.","interpretation":"S530 provides the framework for fire/smoke damage restoration. Compliance supports the justification of specialized cleaning and deodorization charges.","sourceClassification":"INDUSTRY_STANDARD","sourceId":"iicrc-standards","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["iicrc","s530","fire","smoke","standard"]},{"id":"std_irc_building","knowledgeType":"standard","title":"International Residential Code (IRC)","statement":"The IRC is the baseline building code for residential construction in most U.S. jurisdictions. It governs structural, electrical, plumbing, mechanical, and energy requirements.","interpretation":"The IRC establishes minimum code requirements that often trigger upgrade obligations during restoration. Jurisdictions may adopt the IRC with local amendments.","sourceClassification":"REGULATORY","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["irc","building_code","residential","standard"]},{"id":"std_ashrae_90","knowledgeType":"standard","title":"ASHRAE Standard 90.1 — Energy Standard for Buildings","statement":"ASHRAE 90.1 establishes minimum energy efficiency requirements for commercial and residential buildings, affecting HVAC, insulation, and window specifications.","interpretation":"Energy code upgrades during restoration may require ASHRAE 90.1 compliance. This can create legitimate supplement items for code-required upgrades.","sourceClassification":"INDUSTRY_STANDARD","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.75,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["ashrae","energy","efficiency","standard"]},{"id":"risk_no_authorization","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Work Without Written Authorization","statement":"Beginning mitigation or reconstruction without signed authorization from the policyholder creates payment dispute risk.","interpretation":"Always confirm written authorization is on file before work begins. This is a frequently encountered risk factor in the restoration industry.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.9,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","authorization","compliance"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm","mold"]},{"id":"risk_missing_permits","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Unpermitted Work","statement":"Reconstruction work performed without required building permits creates liability exposure and potential coverage issues.","interpretation":"Permits are required for structural, electrical, plumbing, and HVAC work. Verify permit requirements before reconstruction begins.","sourceClassification":"REGULATORY","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","permits","compliance","regulation"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"risk_no_mitigation_docs","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Missing Mitigation Documentation","statement":"Water mitigation performed without drying logs, moisture readings, or equipment documentation faces payment disputes.","interpretation":"Drying documentation is the most frequently disputed item in water mitigation claims. Maintain complete daily logs with timestamps.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"iicrc-standards","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.9,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","mitigation","documentation","drying"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water"]},{"id":"risk_lead_noncompliance","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"EPA Lead RRP Non-Compliance","statement":"Pre-1978 residential work performed without lead-safe practices violates federal regulation and creates significant liability.","interpretation":"EPA fines for Lead RRP violations can reach $37,500 per violation per day. Verify building age and conduct lead testing for pre-1978 structures.","sourceClassification":"REGULATORY","sourceId":"epa-regulations","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.9,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","epa","lead","compliance","pre1978"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"risk_osha_violation","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"OSHA Safety Violation","statement":"Construction work without proper safety measures (fall protection, respiratory protection, electrical safety) triggers OSHA citations.","interpretation":"OSHA violations create financial penalties and potential project shutdowns. Document safety compliance for every job.","sourceClassification":"REGULATORY","sourceId":"osha-construction","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.9,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","osha","safety","compliance"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm","mold"]},{"id":"risk_incomplete_photos","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Incomplete Photo Documentation","statement":"Insufficient photo documentation at any stage reduces the ability to justify scope, support supplements, or resolve disputes.","interpretation":"Photo documentation is the single most controllable factor in claim outcomes. Use date-stamped, room-labeled, wide-and-close-up photo protocols.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","photos","documentation"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm","mold"]},{"id":"risk_no_change_orders","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Undocumented Change Orders","statement":"Scope changes during reconstruction without written change orders result in unrecovered revenue.","interpretation":"Every scope change should have a signed change order before additional work proceeds. This is a primary source of revenue leakage.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","change_order","revenue","documentation"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"risk_missed_deadline","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Missed Claim Deadline","statement":"Failing to meet carrier or regulatory deadlines (proof of loss, supplemental submission, statute of limitations) jeopardizes the claim.","interpretation":"Track all claim deadlines systematically. Set internal deadlines earlier than carrier deadlines to create buffer for follow-up.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","deadline","timing","compliance"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm","mold","flood"]},{"id":"risk_no_preloss_docs","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Missing Pre-Loss Condition Documentation","statement":"Without documentation of the property''s pre-loss condition, disputes about pre-existing damage become difficult to resolve.","interpretation":"When possible, obtain or reference prior inspection photos, maintenance records, or listing photos to establish the pre-loss baseline.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","pre_loss","documentation","baseline"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm","mold"]},{"id":"risk_content_inventory","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Incomplete Content Inventory","statement":"Missing or incomplete content inventory during pack-out creates disputes about contents coverage and value.","interpretation":"Photograph every item before moving it. Maintain chain of custody documentation. Get policyholder sign-off on disposals.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","content","inventory","packout"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","smoke"]},{"id":"risk_missing_weather_data","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Missing Weather Event Correlation","statement":"Weather-related claims without official weather data correlation may face cause-of-loss disputes.","interpretation":"For wind, hail, or storm claims, obtain NOAA or weather station data correlating the loss date with the reported event.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.75,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","weather","documentation","cause_of_loss"],"affectedClaimTypes":["storm","wind","hail"]},{"id":"risk_underbilling","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Underbilling / Missed Billable Work","statement":"Delivered work that is never invoiced, or change orders not added to scope, quietly erodes revenue.","interpretation":"Revenue leakage from underbilling is commonly estimated in the range of 5-15% of total project value (Atlas heuristic based on industry observation). Systematic scope reconciliation helps catch this.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.65,"isInference":true,"status":"active","tags":["risk","revenue","billing","leakage"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"risk_scope_gap","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Carrier Estimate Scope Gap","statement":"Differences between the carrier approved estimate and the actual scope of work represent unrecovered revenue without supplement action.","interpretation":"Scope gaps are the primary driver of supplement activity. Systematic comparison of estimate vs. actual scope identifies revenue opportunities.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","scope","gap","revenue","supplement"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"risk_material_price_inflation","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Material Price Inflation After Estimate","statement":"Material prices increasing between estimate approval and purchase creates cost overruns not covered by the original estimate.","interpretation":"Document current material pricing at time of purchase. Price variance between estimate and purchase may be recoverable through supplements.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.7,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","materials","pricing","inflation"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"risk_supplement_denial","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Supplement Item Denial","statement":"Individual supplement items may be denied due to insufficient documentation, carrier policy limitations, or coverage exclusions.","interpretation":"Denied items may be recoverable through additional documentation, appeal, or escalation. Track each line item''s status separately.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","supplement","denial","revenue"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"risk_shortpayment","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Carrier Short-Payment","statement":"Carriers may pay less than the approved amount due to depreciation holdback, deductible application, or unapproved items.","interpretation":"Short-payments require systematic tracking and follow-up. Depreciation holdbacks may be recoverable upon completion proof.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","payment","shortpay","depreciation"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"risk_hidden_damage","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Hidden Damage Discovery","statement":"Conditions discovered during demolition or restoration that were not visible during initial inspection.","interpretation":"Hidden damage is a legitimate source of scope changes and supplements. Document everything discovered during the work with photos and detailed descriptions.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","hidden_damage","supplement","scope_change"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm","mold"]},{"id":"risk_contractor_dispute","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Subcontractor Performance Dispute","statement":"Subcontractor quality issues, delays, or billing disputes that affect the overall project timeline and budget.","interpretation":"Vet subcontractors before engagement. Maintain written agreements with scope, timeline, and payment terms. Document performance issues immediately.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.75,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","subcontractor","quality","dispute"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"risk_weather_delay","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Weather-Related Work Delay","statement":"Weather conditions preventing scheduled work create timeline extensions and potential additional costs.","interpretation":"Weather delays are generally legitimate and documentable. Include weather delay documentation in the job file to support timeline extensions.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","weather","delay","timeline"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"risk_policyholder_communication","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Poor Policyholder Communication","statement":"Inadequate communication with the policyholder about timeline, scope changes, or costs creates satisfaction issues and dispute risk.","interpretation":"Regular policyholder updates (weekly minimum) prevent misunderstandings. Document all communications and get written approval for scope changes.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","communication","customer","satisfaction"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm","mold"]},{"id":"risk_scope_creep","knowledgeType":"risk_pattern","title":"Scope Creep Without Authorization","statement":"Performing work beyond the approved scope without written authorization creates uncompensable costs.","interpretation":"Stick to the approved scope. Any additional work must be authorized in writing before proceeding. Document scope boundaries clearly.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["risk","scope","authorization","creep"],"affectedClaimTypes":["water","fire","storm"]},{"id":"rev_scope_gap_recovery","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Scope Gap Revenue Recovery","statement":"Scope gaps between the contractor estimate and the carrier approved estimate represent recoverable revenue through the supplement process.","interpretation":"The supplement process is the primary mechanism for recovering revenue that was missed in the original estimate. Quality documentation of each gap with supporting evidence is essential.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","recovery","scope","supplement"]},{"id":"rev_code_upgrade","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Code Upgrade Revenue Recovery","statement":"Building code requirements that necessitate upgrades beyond the original scope can represent recoverable revenue through supplements.","interpretation":"When building codes require upgrades (e.g., ice and water shield, upgraded ventilation), these may be legitimate supplement items supported by regulatory authority. Recovery depends on jurisdiction and carrier policy.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.75,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","recovery","code","regulation"]},{"id":"rev_material_price_variance","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Material Price Variance Recovery","statement":"Material price increases between estimate date and purchase date can create recoverable price variance.","interpretation":"When material prices increase after the estimate is approved, the price difference may be submitted as a supplement item with current pricing documentation. Approval varies by carrier and jurisdiction.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.7,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","recovery","materials","pricing"]},{"id":"rev_hidden_damage","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Hidden Damage Discovery Revenue","statement":"Damage discovered during demolition or restoration that was not visible during the initial inspection can be documented and submitted as a supplement.","interpretation":"Hidden damage is a legitimate source of revenue recovery. Document everything discovered with before/during photos and detailed descriptions before covering it up.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","recovery","hidden_damage","supplement"]},{"id":"rev_change_order_capture","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Change Order Revenue Capture","statement":"Scope changes during reconstruction that are properly documented with signed change orders become billable revenue.","interpretation":"Every scope change is potential revenue. The key is timely documentation with written authorization before the additional work proceeds.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","recovery","change_order","scope"]},{"id":"rev_equipment_days","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Mitigation Equipment Day Recovery","statement":"Equipment days (dehumidifiers, air movers, fans) deployed during water mitigation are billable per-day charges supported by drying documentation.","interpretation":"Equipment day charges are a significant portion of water mitigation revenue. Proper drying logs with timestamps and moisture readings justify each day of equipment deployment.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"iicrc-standards","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.9,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","mitigation","equipment","drying"]},{"id":"rev_content_replacement","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Content Replacement Revenue","statement":"Damaged contents that cannot be restored represent replacement cost coverage that should be fully documented and billed.","interpretation":"Content replacement is often under-claimed. Ensure every damaged item is inventoried, photographed, and valued at replacement cost where the policy provides RCV coverage.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","content","replacement","coverage"]},{"id":"rev_overhead_profit","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Overhead & Profit Recovery","statement":"Overhead and profit (O&P) are typically 10% + 10% on top of the sub cost and should be included in estimates and supplements where applicable.","interpretation":"O&P is legitimate business cost recovery. Some carriers require specific documentation or trigger O&P approval at certain thresholds (e.g., when 3+ trades are involved).","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","overhead","profit","O&P"]},{"id":"rev_depreciation_holdback","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Depreciation Holdback Recovery","statement":"ACV (Actual Cash Value) policies include depreciation holdback that is released upon proof of completed repairs.","interpretation":"Depreciation holdback can represent a significant portion of the total claim value. Submit proof of completion promptly to release the holdback.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","depreciation","holdback","ACV"]},{"id":"rev_permit_fee_recovery","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Permit Fee Recovery","statement":"Building permit fees required for the restoration work are generally recoverable as part of the insurance claim.","interpretation":"Include permit fees in the estimate. Some carriers automatically approve permit costs; others require specific documentation.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","permits","fees","recovery"]},{"id":"rev_matching_item","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Matching Item Recovery","statement":"When damaged materials cannot be matched (flooring, siding, cabinetry), the carrier may be required to replace the full area to achieve a reasonable match.","interpretation":"Matching requirements vary by state and carrier. Document the inability to match and research the applicable matching requirements in the jurisdiction.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.75,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","matching","siding","flooring"]},{"id":"rev_demand_letter","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Demand Letter / Appraisal","statement":"When supplement negotiations fail, a formal demand letter or appraisal process can resolve disputed amounts.","interpretation":"Demand letters and appraisal are escalation mechanisms. They should be used after good-faith supplement negotiation has been exhausted.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.7,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","demand","appraisal","dispute"]},{"id":"rev_bad_faith","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Bad Faith Claim Considerations","statement":"When carriers unreasonably deny, delay, or underpay claims, policyholders may have bad faith claims that can recover additional damages.","interpretation":"Bad faith claims are legal proceedings and should be referred to appropriate legal counsel. Atlas can identify patterns that may indicate bad faith behavior.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.65,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","bad_faith","legal","dispute"]},{"id":"rev_standing_inventory","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Standing Inventory / Stored Contents","statement":"Contents in storage during restoration incur storage charges that are generally billable as part of the claim.","interpretation":"Storage charges are legitimate costs. Document the storage period, facility, and monthly charges. Include in the content claim package.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","content","storage","inventory"]},{"id":"rev_emergency_service","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Emergency Service Premium Recovery","statement":"Emergency mitigation services (after-hours, weekend, holiday) may carry premium rates that are recoverable when the emergency was time-sensitive.","interpretation":"Emergency service rates should be documented in the work authorization. The urgency and timing of the response support the premium charges.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.8,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","emergency","premium","mitigation"]},{"id":"rev_xactimate_optimization","knowledgeType":"revenue_concept","title":"Xactimate Line Item Optimization","statement":"Using the correct Xactimate line items with appropriate modifiers and unit pricing ensures the estimate accurately reflects the actual scope of work.","interpretation":"Xactimate expertise directly affects revenue. Under-pricing or using incorrect line items leaves money on the table. Regular pricing updates ensure current local rates.","sourceClassification":"ATLAS_CURATED","sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","industry":"insurance restoration","jurisdiction":"United States","confidence":0.85,"isInference":false,"status":"active","tags":["revenue","xactimate","pricing","optimization"]}]'::jsonb,
  '[{"sourceId":"fema-flood-insurance","sourceName":"FEMA National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP)","organization":"US FEMA","authorityTier":"tier1_primary","sourceType":"regulation","canonicalUrl":"https://www.fema.gov/flood-insurance","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"iicrc-standards","sourceName":"IICRC Restoration Standards (S500, S520, S530)","organization":"Institute of Inspection, Cleaning and Restoration Certification","authorityTier":"tier2_authoritative","sourceType":"standard","canonicalUrl":"https://www.iicrc.org/standards/","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"osha-construction","sourceName":"OSHA Construction Standards (29 CFR 1926)","organization":"US OSHA","authorityTier":"tier1_primary","sourceType":"regulation","canonicalUrl":"https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1926","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"epa-regulations","sourceName":"EPA Lead RRP Rule & Asbestos Regulations","organization":"US EPA","authorityTier":"tier1_primary","sourceType":"regulation","canonicalUrl":"https://www.epa.gov/lead","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"insurance-industry-practice","sourceName":"U.S. Insurance Restoration Industry Practice & Guidance","organization":"Atlas (curated from industry sources)","authorityTier":"tier3_industry","sourceType":"curated","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"atlas-curated","sourceName":"Atlas Industry Knowledge — Curated","organization":"Atlas","authorityTier":"tier3_industry","sourceType":"curated","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"atlas-evidence-model","sourceName":"Atlas Evidence Requirements Model","organization":"Atlas","authorityTier":"tier3_industry","sourceType":"curated","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"atlas-professional-guidance","sourceName":"Atlas Industry Operational Guidance","organization":"Atlas","authorityTier":"tier3_industry","sourceType":"curated","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"iicrc-s500","sourceName":"IICRC S500 — Standard for Professional Water Damage Restoration","organization":"IICRC","authorityTier":"tier2_authoritative","sourceType":"standard","canonicalUrl":"https://www.iicrc.org/page/560/s500-standard","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"iicrc-s520","sourceName":"IICRC S520 — Standard for Professional Mold Remediation","organization":"IICRC","authorityTier":"tier2_authoritative","sourceType":"standard","canonicalUrl":"https://www.iicrc.org/page/561/s520-standard","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"osha-construction-detail","sourceName":"OSHA — Construction Standards Detail (29 CFR 1926)","organization":"US OSHA","authorityTier":"tier1_primary","sourceType":"regulation","status":"active"},{"sourceId":"epa-lead-rrp","sourceName":"EPA — Lead Renovation, Repair & Painting Rule (40 CFR 745)","organization":"US EPA","authorityTier":"tier1_primary","sourceType":"regulation","status":"active"}]'::jsonb,
